  "slides": [
    {
      "page_title": "Why Dataclasses",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "1. What is it?",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Plain classes need hand-written `__init__`, `__repr__`, `__eq__` for every data holder",
            "==@dataclass== (PEP 557, Python 3.7+) reads class-level annotations",
            "It then generates those methods for you",
            "Decorator returns the **same** class object, edited in place",
            "Not a metaclass, not a base class"
          ],
          "style": "star",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "2. Boilerplate vs Dataclass",
          "ink": "purple"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "python",
          "lines": [
            "# by hand: 9 lines",
            "class P:",
            "  def __init__(self, x, y):",
            "    self.x = x; self.y = y",
            "  # + __repr__ + __eq__ ...",
            "",
            "# with the decorator",
            "@dataclass",
            "class P: x: int; y: int"
          ],
          "note": "same class, 2 lines instead of 9"
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "sticky",
          "title": "Remember",
          "text": "The decorator **writes code** for you; nothing more magical than that."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "3. Example: Point",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "python",
          "lines": [
            "@dataclass",
            "class Point:",
            "  x: int",
            "  y: int",
            "",
            "Point(1, 2)   # Point(x=1, y=2)",
            "Point('a', None)  # works fine"
          ],
          "note": "annotation is NOT enforced"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "Example : `repr(Point(1, 2))` gives 'Point(x=1, y=2)'. Hints are ==documentation==, not checks.",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "pink"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "4. What becomes a field?",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Declaration",
            "Field?",
            "Why"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "`x: int`",
              "yes",
              "annotated"
            ],
            [
              "`x: int = 0`",
              "yes",
              "annotated + default"
            ],
            [
              "`tag = 1`",
              "**no**",
              "plain class attr"
            ],
            [
              "`tag: int = 1`",
              "yes",
              "annotation makes it one"
            ]
          ],
          "frame": "dashed"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "5. Field order = init order",
          "ink": "purple"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Fields follow **definition order**",
            "That order fixes the positional args of `__init__`",
            "Generated `__init__` assigns each to `self`, in order",
            "There is **no validation** at all"
          ],
          "style": "number",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "purple"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "7. When to use it",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Class mainly **holds data**: use dataclass",
            "Behavior or invariants dominate: use a normal class",
            "Expecting type checks at runtime: they never happen"
          ],
          "style": "check",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "Summary",
          "text": "`@dataclass` turns annotated names into fields and generates `__init__`, `__repr__`, `__eq__`. No enforcement. Next: what exactly gets generated."
        }
      ],
      "hero": "python",
      "subtitle": "The Boilerplate Problem"
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Introspection and Common Mistakes",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "python",
          "lines": [
            "@dataclass",
            "class U:",
            "  name: str",
            "  age: int",
            "",
            "U('Ann', 30)  # name, then age"
          ],
          "note": "order of definition = argument order"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "1. Introspection",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Call",
            "Returns"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "`fields(obj)`",
              "tuple of Field objects"
            ],
            [
              "`is_dataclass(Point)`",
              "True (class)"
            ],
            [
              "`is_dataclass(Point(1,2))`",
              "True (instance)"
            ],
            [
              "`is_dataclass(int)`",
              "False"
            ]
          ],
          "frame": "dashed"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "python",
          "lines": [
            "from dataclasses import fields",
            "",
            "for f in fields(Point):",
            "    print(f.name, f.type)",
            "# x <class 'int'>",
            "# y <class 'int'>"
          ],
          "note": "works on class or instance"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`fields()` returns **Field** objects, not values",
            "Each Field holds name, type, default",
            "`is_dataclass` accepts a class or an instance"
          ],
          "style": "dot",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "2. Dataclass vs Plain Class",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Task",
            "Plain class",
            "Dataclass"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "`__init__`",
              "write it",
              "generated"
            ],
            [
              "`__repr__`",
              "write it",
              "generated"
            ],
            [
              "`__eq__`",
              "write it",
              "generated"
            ],
            [
              "Validation",
              "your code",
              "none either"
            ]
          ],
          "frame": "dashed"
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "tip",
          "title": "Tip",
          "text": "Reach for ==@dataclass== whenever you catch yourself typing `self.x = x` three times."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "3. Common Mistakes",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Expecting `Point('a', None)` to **raise** an error",
            "Writing `tag = 1` and expecting a field",
            "Believing the decorator makes a new base class",
            "Forgetting field order sets the argument order"
          ],
          "style": "cross",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "python",
          "lines": [
            "@dataclass",
            "class Tag:",
            "    n: int = 1   # field",
            "    label = 'x'  # NOT a field",
            "",
            "Tag(2, 'y')  # TypeError"
          ],
          "note": "unannotated names are skipped"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "4. Building Classes Dynamically",
          "ink": "purple"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "python",
          "lines": [
            "from dataclasses import make_dataclass",
            "",
            "P = make_dataclass('P', ['x', 'y'])",
            "P(1, 2)  # P(x=1, y=2)"
          ],
          "note": "untyped names get type Any"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`make_dataclass` builds a class at runtime",
            "Field names can be plain strings",
            "Pass `(name, type)` pairs to add types"
          ],
          "style": "dot",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "purple"
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "Remember",
          "text": "Annotation makes the field. The type itself is only a hint."
        }
      ],
      "hero": "python",
